"Intro astronomy (GenEd, algebra); majors astronomy (rotational energy, magnetic dipole braking)",
"A neutron star packs more than a solar mass into a ball about {#} km across, as dense as an atomic nucleus: about {#} × {#}¹⁷ kg/m³.",
"A collapsing core keeps its spin and its magnetic flux. Shrink it by a factor f and it spins f² times faster and its field grows f² times stronger.",
"A pulsar is a spinning neutron star whose beams, along its magnetic axis, sweep past Earth like a lighthouse. The time between pulses is the spin period. If neither beam crosses our line of sight we see no pulses, though the star is still there.",
"Pulsars slow down as they lose spin energy. The Crab pulsar's lost spin energy, about {#} Suns, is what keeps the Crab Nebula glowing.",
"Students should know the density formula ρ = M/V and the idea that a spinning skater speeds up when she pulls her arms in. Turn on <strong>Projector</strong>. The three views are <strong>Collapse</strong>, <strong>Lighthouse</strong> and <strong>Spin down</strong>. Drag the scene, or use the arrow keys on it, to turn the star; the time scale is written in the scene.",
"<strong>A core {#} km in radius turning once a day</strong> is the lesson's Ex27. Ask Q2 first, then press <strong>Play</strong>. The radius is drawn on a log scale. At the end: shrink factor {#}, spin {#} times faster, P = {#} s / {#} = {#} s, and the field {#} times stronger. The graph shows P falling as R² and B rising as {#}/R².",
"<strong>If the Sun collapsed</strong> squeezes the whole Sun to {#} km: it would need to spin faster than a ball that size can hold together. Real cores lose most of their spin on the way in.",
"<strong>Heaviest neutron star known</strong>: PSR J0740+{#}, {#} M_sun in about {#} km. Read the density and compare with an atomic nucleus.",
"Switch to <strong>Lighthouse</strong>. <strong>Bell's first pulsar: one pulse a turn</strong>, shown in real time: a pulse every {#} s on the radio trace below. <strong>Both beams cross us: two pulses</strong> gives a main pulse and an interpulse, as the Crab shows. <strong>The beams miss us</strong>: no pulses at all. Tilt the beams with the sliders and watch <strong>Pulses each turn</strong>.",
"Switch to <strong>Spin down</strong>. <strong>The Crab pulsar</strong>: P = {#} ms, slowing by {#} ns a day. Its characteristic age P/{#}Ṗ is {#} years; the supernova was seen in {#}. Read the energy lost: {#} × {#}³¹ W, about {#} Suns. <strong>Bell's first pulsar, slowing</strong> is an old pulsar: about {#} million years by P/{#}Ṗ, with a weaker field.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> A neutron star has {#} M_sun (M_sun = {#} × {#}³⁰ kg) and a radius of {#} km. Find its density. How does it compare with an atomic nucleus, about {#} × {#}¹⁷ kg/m³?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> A star's core of radius {#} km turns once a day. It collapses to a neutron star of radius {#} km, keeping its spin. What is the new period? Check with <strong>Play</strong>.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> A core of radius {#} km turning once every {#} hours collapses to {#} km. What is the new period? If its magnetic field was {#} T, what is it after the collapse?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> The Crab pulsar spins about {#} times a second and has a radius of about {#} km. What is its period, and how fast does its equator move? Compare with the speed of light.",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> Pulsar pulses are perfectly regular. What sets the time between pulses? In <strong>Lighthouse</strong>, why do some setups give two pulses each turn, and <strong>The beams miss us</strong> none?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> Astronomers know of a few thousand pulsars. Use <strong>The beams miss us</strong> to explain why there must be many more neutron stars than pulsars we can see.",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> The Crab pulsar slows by {#} ns every day. About how many years until its period has doubled from {#} ms to {#} ms, if it kept slowing at today's rate? (The real slowing gets gentler as it slows.) Compare with its characteristic age τ = P/{#}Ṗ.",
"<strong>\"Pulsars pulse: the star brightens and dims.\"</strong> The beams are steady; they sweep past us as the star turns.",
"<strong>\"Every neutron star is a pulsar.\"</strong> Only those whose beams cross our line of sight, and only while they spin fast enough to make beams.",
"<strong>\"The collapse makes the star spin, as if something pushes it.\"</strong> It already spun slowly. Keeping its angular momentum while shrinking makes it spin faster.",
"<strong>\"A neutron star is a big atom.\"</strong> It is held up by neutron degeneracy pressure and the strong force, and it is gravitationally bound, not bound like a nucleus.",
